## WHAT THIS CODEBASE IS, AND HOW TO NAVIGATE IT

This is the Metrolinx / GO Transit `next.gotransit.com` site: a Next.js + React + TypeScript
application, styled with Tailwind, tested with Jest (`npm test`) and React Testing Library.
Its layout idioms, which should shape where you look and what you prescribe:

- Page-level UI lives under `src/components/pages/<PageName>/` — the checkout surface the survey
  found is `src/components/pages/CheckoutPage`. A component's tests sit beside it in a
  `__tests__/` subfolder next to the component file (e.g. a component `Foo.tsx` has
  `__tests__/Foo.spec.tsx`). If your fix changes validation or comparison behaviour, the
  matching `.spec.tsx` in that same `__tests__/` folder is a legitimate second fix site —
  decide honestly whether it must be EDITED (new/changed assertions ⇒ changeRequired true) or
  merely re-run (changeRequired false).
- Forms in this project are built with `react-hook-form`; field-level rules are usually declared
  inline on the field registration (`validate`, `required`, `pattern`) rather than in a separate
  schema module. A cross-field check therefore normally lives in the component that owns both
  fields, not in a shared util. READ the actual registration with `show` before you quote it —
  the `validate` callback's real parameter names and the value it compares against are exactly
  the kind of thing that gets reconstructed from memory and rejected.
- Copy and error messages are translated: this codeline uses `next-i18next` / `react-i18next`,
  and user-facing strings normally arrive through a translation function rather than as literals.
  Do not prescribe a hard-coded English message if the surrounding code translates; and do not
  claim a translation key exists unless a tool showed it to you.
- `lodash.*` helpers are installed as individual packages (`lodash.isempty`, `lodash.isstring`,
  and so on) — there is no bare `lodash`. If your fix imports one, name the exact package
  (e.g. "lodash.isstring") in `requiredPackages`, and only if this codeline actually declares it.
  A plain JavaScript string method needs no package at all: `requiredPackages: []` is the
  expected answer for most fixes here.

Traps specific to this stack that a generic prompt would not warn you about:

- Validation runs in the BROWSER for a react-hook-form field rule. Next.js also renders
  components on the SERVER, so a component file is not automatically `runsIn: "browser"`;
  a submit handler or a `validate` callback fires on the client, while `getServerSideProps` or
  an API route under `pages/api` does not. Choose `runsIn` from what the specific FUNCTION does.
- String comparison semantics are the substance of this kind of ticket. If the fix hinges on
  case-insensitive matching, NAME the exact transformation and the exact operator you are
  prescribing (for example `.toLowerCase()` applied to BOTH sides of a `===`), and copy the
  existing comparison expression verbatim from `show` output. "Compare case-insensitively"
  without the literal method is not implementable — this is machine-checked.
- Trimming and case-folding are different changes. Do not silently bundle whitespace trimming
  into a case-sensitivity fix unless the ticket asks for it; say precisely which one you mean.

Commands this project runs, for grounding only — do NOT run them, and do not prescribe a fix
whose verification requires anything else: tests are `npm test` (Jest), and the repo type-checks
and lints as part of its normal build. A husky pre-commit hook runs lint-staged and rejects
commits unless the branch name matches `^(feature|bugfix|tech|release|hotfix)/[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+(/[a-z0-9-]+)?$`,
and a commit-msg hook runs commitlint; that constrains the implementer, not your investigation,
so do not put hook or branch concerns in your fix sites.

